/** @page command_line_processing qore Executable Command-Line Processing

    @tableofcontents

    This section describes command-line processing for the \c qore binary; see Qore::GetOpt for the class allowing for command-line processing in %Qore code

    %Qore will scan the command-line for the options in the following table. Arguments after the script name will be passed to the script in the global <tt>ARGV</tt> variable as a list of options. <tt>ARGV[0]</tt> will be the first option and will not be the script name. If no script name is given and the <tt>--exec</tt> option is not used, then %Qore code is read from standard input.  The <tt>QORE_ARGV</tt> variable will have the original %Qore command-line in it, however

    Usage: <tt>qore [</tt><em>options</em><tt>] [</tt><em>script_file_name</em><tt>]</tt>

    @note When no script file is given and the \c --exec option is not used, %Qore will automatically enter
    interactive REPL (Read-Eval-Print Loop) mode if standard input is a terminal. Use \c --no-repl to disable
    this behavior and read from standard input instead.

    <b>Parse Option Command-Line Parameters</b>
    |!Long Param|!Short|!Description
    |<tt>--define=</tt><em>arg</em>|\c -D|Creates and optionally sets a value for a parse define
    |<tt>--load=</tt><em>arg</em>|\c -l|Loads a module immediately. The argument can be a module/feature name or an absolute path to the module
    |<tt>--enable-debug</tt>|\c -G|Enables @@assert and @@debug statements; equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_ENABLE_DEBUG and the @ref enable-debug "%enable-debug" directive
    |<tt>--modern</tt>|\c -M|Enables the recommended parse option set; equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_MODERN and the @ref modern "%modern" directive
    |<tt>--no-global-vars</tt>|n/a|Disallows the use of global variables. Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_GLOBAL_VARS and the @ref no-global-vars "%no-global-vars" directive
    |<tt>--no-subroutine-defs</tt>|\c -S|Disallows subroutine (function) definitions. Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_SUBROUTINE_DEFS and the @ref no-subroutine-defs "%no-subroutine-defs" directive
    |<tt>--no-thread-control</tt>|\c -R|Disallows any thread control operations (@ref background "background operator" and @ref thread_exit "thread_exit statement", for example). Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL and the @ref no-thread-control "%no-thread-control" directive
    |<tt>--no-thread-classes</tt>|n/a|Disallows access to thread classes (for example, the @ref Qore::Thread::Mutex "Mutex" class, @ref Qore::Thread::Gate "Gate" class, @ref Qore::Thread::Queue "Queue" class, etc). Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CLASSES and the @ref no-thread-classes "%no-thread-classes" directive
    |<tt>--no-threads</tt>|\c -T|Disallows access to both thread control and thread classes (equivalent to the \c --no-thread-control and \c --no-thread-classes options documented above). Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREADS and the @ref no-threads "%no-threads" directive
    |<tt>--no-top-level</tt>|\c -L|Disallows top level code. Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_TOP_LEVEL_STATEMENTS and the @ref no-top-level "%no-top-level" directive
    |<tt>--no-class-defs</tt>|n/a|Disallows class definitions. Equivalent to @ref Qore::PO_NO_CLASS_DEFS and the @ref no-class-defs "%no-class-defs" directive
    |<tt>--no-namespace-defs</tt>|n/a|Disallows new namespace definitions. Equivalent to @ref Qore::PO_NO_NAMESPACE_DEFS and the @ref no-namespace-defs "%no-namespace-defs" directive
    |<tt>--no-external-process</tt>|\c -E|Disallows any access to external processes (with system(), backquote(), exec(), the @ref backquote_operator "backquote operator", etc). Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_PROCESS and the @ref no-external-process "%no-external-process" directive
    |<tt>--no-process-control</tt>|\c -P|Disallows access to functions that would affect the current process (exit(), fork(), etc). Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_PROCESS_CONTROL and the @ref no-process-control "%no-process-control" directive
    |<tt>--no-filesystem</tt>|\c -F|Disallows access to the local filesystem; puts the @ref no-filesystem "%no-filesystem" parse option in effect. Equivalent to parse option code @ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM and the @ref no-filesystem "%no-filesystem" parse directive
    |<tt>--no-constant-defs</tt>|n/a|Disallows constant definitions. Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_CONSTANT_DEFS and the @ref no-constant-defs "%no-constant-defs" directive
    |<tt>--no-network</tt>|\c -Y|Disallows access to the network; puts the @ref no-network "%no-network" parse option in effect. Equivalent to parse option code @ref Qore::PO_NO_NETWORK and the @ref no-network "%no-network" parse directive
    |<tt>--no-new</tt>|\c -N|Disallows use of the @ref new "new operator". Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_NEW and the @ref no-new "%no-new" directive
    |<tt>--no-database</tt>|\c -D|Disallows use of database functionality. Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_DATABASE and the @ref no-database "%no-database" directive
    |<tt>--no-child-restrictions</tt>|\c -I|Allows child program objects to have parse option restrictions that are not a strict subset of the parents. Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_NO_CHILD_PO_RESTRICTIONS and the @ref no-child-restrictions "%no-child-restrictions" directive
    |<tt>--require-our</tt>|\c -O|Requires global variables to be declared with our prior to use (similar to perl's <tt>use strict vars</tt> pragma). Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_REQUIRE_OUR and the @ref require-our "%require-our" directive
    |<tt>--lock-options</tt>|\c -K|Prohibits further changes to parse options (equivalent to the @ref lock-options "%lock-options" directive)
    |<tt>--lock-warnings</tt>|\c -A|Prohibits further changes to the warning mask. Equivalent to parse option @ref Qore::PO_LOCK_WARNINGS and the @ref lock-warnings "%lock-warnings" directive
    |<tt>--enable-all-warnings</tt>|\c -W|Enables all non-strict @ref warnings "warnings". Equivalent to the @ref enable-all-warnings "%enable-all-warnings" directive
    |<tt>--enable-warning=</tt><em>arg</em>|\c -w|Enables the named @ref warnings "warning". Equivalent to the @ref enable-warning "%enable-warning" directive
    |<tt>--strict-warnings</tt>|n/a|Enables strict warnings (ambiguous overload/call resolution)
    |<tt>--list-warnings</tt>|\c -i|Lists all valid @ref warnings "warnings" in %Qore and exits immediately

    <b>Miscellaneous Command-Line Parameters</b>
    |!Long Param|!Short|!Description
    |<tt>--disable-gc</tt>|\c -g|Disables the garbage collector
    |<tt>--exec=</tt><em>arg</em>|\c -e|parses and executes the argument text as a %Qore program. If this option is specified then any script given on the command-line will be ignored
    |<tt>--exec-class[=</tt><em>arg</em><tt>]</tt>|\c -x|instantiates the class with the same name as the program (with the directory path and extension stripped); also turns on --no-top-level. If the program is read from <tt>stdin</tt> or from the command line, an argument must be given specifying the class name
    |<tt>--interactive</tt>|n/a|Forces interactive REPL mode, even when stdin is not a terminal or when input is being piped
    |<tt>--no-repl</tt>|n/a|Disables automatic REPL mode when stdin is a terminal; forces reading from stdin instead
    |<tt>--show-module-errors</tt>|\c -m|Shows any errors loading %Qore modules
    |<tt>--charset=</tt><em>arg</em>|\c -c|Sets the @ref default_encoding "default character encoding" for the program
    |<tt>--show-charset=</tt><em>arg</em>|\c -s|Shows a list of all known @ref character_encoding "character encodings"
    |<tt>--show-aliases</tt>|\c -a|Shows a list of all known @ref character_encoding "character encoding" aliases
    |<tt>--help</tt>|\c -h|Shows help text
    |<tt>--version</tt>|\c -v|Shows %Qore library version information and exits

    <b>Execution Mode Command-Line Parameters</b>

    These options control how %Qore code is executed. See @ref execution_modes for detailed information.

    |!Long Param|!Short|!Description
    |<tt>--exec-mode=</tt><em>arg</em>|n/a|Sets the @ref execution_modes "execution mode": \c ast (AST interpreter), \c ir (IR bytecode interpreter), \c jit (LLVM JIT compilation), or \c tiered (adaptive compilation). The default is \c tiered for @ref modern "%modern" code and \c ast otherwise
    |<tt>--jit-ir-threshold=</tt><em>N</em>|n/a|In @ref exec_mode_tiered "tiered" mode, number of calls before promoting an AST-tier function variant to IR (default: 100)
    |<tt>--jit-jit-threshold=</tt><em>N</em>|n/a|In @ref exec_mode_tiered "tiered" mode, number of calls before promoting a function to JIT (default: 1000)

    <b>AOT Compilation Command-Line Parameters for \c qore</b>

    These options enable basic @ref exec_mode_aot "ahead-of-time compilation" from
    the \c qore executable.  The dedicated @ref qcc_tool "qcc" compiler provides
    the full AOT object, module, archive, link, and inspection workflow.

    |!Long Param|!Short|!Description
    |<tt>--compile</tt>|n/a|Compile the script to a standalone native executable
    |<tt>--compile-module</tt>|n/a|Compile a \c .qm module to a binary module (\c .qmod)
    |<tt>--output=</tt><em>path</em>|\c -o|Specify output path for compiled binary (default: script name without extension)
    |<tt>--strip-source</tt>|n/a|Remove source code from compiled binary (for IP protection)
    |<tt>--opt-level=</tt><em>N</em>|n/a|LLVM optimization level 0-3 (default: 2)
    |<tt>--static</tt>|n/a|Static link single-source executables against \c libqore_static.a (requires static library build)
    |<tt>--target=</tt><em>triple</em>|n/a|Cross-compilation target triple (e.g., \c x86_64-linux-gnu)

    <b>\c qcc AOT Compiler Parameters</b>

    \c qcc is the preferred compiler for AOT build integration and diagnostics.
    It supports all \c qore AOT use cases plus \c .qo object files, \c .qoa
    archives, generated link-mode executables, dependency files, source stubs,
    parser defines, and metadata inspection.

    |!Param|!Description
    |<tt>-o</tt>, <tt>--output=</tt><em>file</em>|Output path
    |<tt>-O</tt>, <tt>--opt-level=</tt><em>N</em>|LLVM optimization level 0-3, default 3
    |<tt>-m</tt>, <tt>--module</tt>|Compile a user module to \c .qmod
    |<tt>-c</tt>, <tt>--compile-only</tt>|Compile to a \c .qo relocatable object
    |<tt>--context=</tt><em>dir</em>|Context directory for per-file or split-module compilation
    |<tt>-L</tt> <em>dir</em>|Preload sibling \c .qo declarations from a directory
    |<tt>--output-dir=</tt><em>dir</em>|Output directory for batch \c -c builds
    |<tt>--depfile-dir=</tt><em>dir</em>|Write one dependency file per batch output into <em>dir</em>
    |<tt>--batch-build-sidecars</tt>|Write incremental-build index, manifest, status, dependency, and stamp sidecars for every batch output; requires \c --depfile-dir
    |<tt>-l</tt>, <tt>--load=</tt><em>module</em>|Load a module before parsing sources
    |<tt>--stub=</tt><em>file</em>|Preload declaration-only source supplied by a host
    |<tt>--define=</tt><em>name</em><tt>[=</tt><em>value</em><tt>]</tt>|Set parser defines before parsing
    |<tt>--parse-option=</tt><em>name</em>|Set a parse option before parsing
    |<tt>--depfile=</tt><em>file</em>|Emit a Make-format dependency file
    |<tt>--save-temps</tt>|Keep temporary \c .qo and glue files from one-shot multi-source executable mode
    |<tt>--from-objects</tt>|Aggregate \c .qo inputs into a \c .qmod with \c -m
    |<tt>-a</tt>, <tt>--archive</tt>|Create a \c .qoa archive from \c .qo inputs
    |<tt>--include-source</tt>|Embed source text in the artifact
    |<tt>--strip-source</tt>|Omit source text; this is the \c qcc default
    |<tt>--strip-debug-info</tt>|Omit DWARF debug information
    |<tt>-g</tt>|Emit DWARF debug information
    |<tt>--time-trace[=</tt><em>path</em><tt>]</tt>|Emit Chrome-format LLVM timing trace
    |<tt>--big-fn-threshold=</tt><em>N</em>|Compile very large functions with \c OptimizeNone, default 200
    |<tt>-e</tt>, <tt>--entry=</tt><em>fn</em>|Entry function for generated one-shot or link-mode \c main()
    |<tt>-r</tt>, <tt>--warnings-are-errors</tt>|Treat Qore parser warnings as fatal compile errors
    |<tt>--dump-info</tt>|Inspect embedded AOT metadata without executing the artifact
    |<tt>--dump-symbols</tt>|Include an nm-like symbol table in dump output
    |<tt>--dump-sections</tt>|Include object and AOT section tables in dump output

    AOT compilation requires @ref modern "%modern" source semantics. Modern
    script and application sources should use the \c .qr extension; \c .q keeps
    legacy defaults unless the source contains an explicit \c %%modern directive.
    Other non-\c .q source extensions, such as \c .qc and \c .qm, enable
    \c %%modern by default.

    A generated AOT executable invokes the Qore function named by \c --entry
    (or \c -e), which defaults to \c main. This differs from running a parsed
    script with \c qore: top-level executable statements do not replace the
    entry function. The input must therefore define \c main(), or define the
    function explicitly selected with \c --entry; otherwise the executable
    exits with a \c NO-FUNCTION exception.

    Multi-file applications can be compiled directly to an executable:

    @code{.sh}
qcc -o myapp src/lib.qr src/main.qr
    @endcode

    Build systems should normally use the explicit object workflow, compiling
    \c .qr sources to \c .qo objects and linking the objects into an executable:

    @code{.sh}
qcc -c --output-dir=build/aot src/lib.qr src/main.qr
qcc -o myapp build/aot/*.qo
    @endcode

    <b>Diagnostic Command-Line Parameters</b>

    These options make %Qore's parse-time diagnostics (errors, warnings, and the
    "did you mean ...?" suggestions described in @ref identifier_suggestions) available in
    machine-readable or rendered form, primarily for editors, build tools, and
    AI-driven coding assistants. Both options are <em>parse-only</em>: %Qore parses
    the source, emits the diagnostics, and exits <b>without executing the program</b>.
    The process exit status is \c 2 if any parse error was reported and \c 0
    otherwise (so a clean parse, or one with only warnings, exits \c 0). Each option
    has an equivalent environment variable (see @ref environment_variables) for
    tooling that cannot control the command line.

    |!Long Param|!Short|!Description
    |<tt>--diag-format=</tt><em>fmt</em>|n/a|Parse only and emit the collected parse diagnostics in the given format to \c stdout, suppressing the normal human-readable error/warning output. The only supported format is \c json. Equivalent to the \c QORE_DIAG_FORMAT environment variable
    |<tt>--code-frames</tt>|n/a|Parse only and render the collected parse diagnostics as Rust-style code frames (a <tt>file:line:col</tt> header, the offending source line, and a caret span) to \c stderr, suppressing the normal output. Equivalent to the \c QORE_CODE_FRAMES environment variable

    @section diag_json_format JSON Diagnostic Format

    With <tt>--diag-format=json</tt>, %Qore prints a JSON array to \c stdout in
    which each element is an object whose fields correspond exactly to the
    @ref Qore::ParseDiagnosticInfo "ParseDiagnosticInfo" hashdecl, so the
    command-line emitter and the embedded
    @ref Qore::Program::getParseDiagnostics() "Program::getParseDiagnostics()" API
    return the same information. The fields are \c severity (<tt>"error"</tt> or
    <tt>"warning"</tt>), \c code (the legacy exception or warning code), \c id
    (a stable id for the exact semantic condition), \c warningCode (the
    @ref warnings "warning bit" for warnings, \c null for errors), \c file,
    \c source, \c startLine, \c endLine, \c startColumn, \c endColumn (a column of
    \c -1 means the column is unknown), \c message, \c hint, \c suggestions,
    \c facts, \c relatedLocations, and \c fixes.  A fix declares an
    <tt>applicability</tt> of <tt>machine-applicable</tt> or
    <tt>review-required</tt>; tools must not apply review-required fixes without
    review.  Legacy diagnostics that do not yet provide richer metadata use
    their \c code as \c id and return empty metadata fields.

    For example, given the following file \c bad.q with two errors (an undefined
    class and a function-name typo):

    @code
%modern
class Widget {}

int sub calculateTotal(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

Widget w = new widget();
int result = calculateTotl(2, 3);
    @endcode

    running:

    @code{.sh}
qore --diag-format=json bad.q
    @endcode

    prints (formatted here for readability; the real output is a single line):

    @code{.json}
[
  {
    "severity": "error",
    "code": "PARSE-EXCEPTION",
    "id": "PARSE-EXCEPTION",
    "warningCode": null,
    "file": "bad.q",
    "source": null,
    "startLine": 8,
    "endLine": 8,
    "startColumn": 12,
    "endColumn": 24,
    "message": "reference to undefined class 'widget'; did you mean 'Widget'? (check capitalization)",
    "hint": null,
    "suggestions": [],
    "facts": {},
    "relatedLocations": [],
    "fixes": []
  },
  {
    "severity": "error",
    "code": "PARSE-EXCEPTION",
    "id": "PARSE-EXCEPTION",
    "warningCode": null,
    "file": "bad.q",
    "source": null,
    "startLine": 9,
    "endLine": 9,
    "startColumn": -1,
    "endColumn": -1,
    "message": "function 'calculateTotl()' cannot be found; did you mean 'calculateTotal'?",
    "hint": null,
    "suggestions": [],
    "facts": {},
    "relatedLocations": [],
    "fixes": []
  }
]
    @endcode

    Warnings are captured the same way once they are enabled (for example with
    \c -W / @ref enable-all-warnings). Given a non-exhaustive @ref switch "switch"
    over an @ref enums "enum" (see @ref non-exhaustive-switch):

    @code
%modern
%enable-all-warnings
enum Color {
    RED = 0,
    GREEN = 1,
    BLUE = 2,
}

int sub describe(Color c) {
    switch (c) {
        case Color::RED: return 1;
        case Color::GREEN: return 2;
    }
    return 0;
}
    @endcode

    @code{.sh}
qore --diag-format=json warn.q
    @endcode

    emits a single warning element and exits \c 0 (a warning is not an error):

    @code{.json}
[
  {
    "severity": "warning",
    "code": "NON-EXHAUSTIVE-SWITCH",
    "id": "NON-EXHAUSTIVE-SWITCH",
    "warningCode": 1048576,
    "file": "warn.q",
    "source": null,
    "startLine": 10,
    "endLine": 13,
    "startColumn": -1,
    "endColumn": -1,
    "message": "switch over enum 'Color' does not handle 1 of its values (BLUE); add the missing case or a default: label",
    "hint": "add the missing enum cases or an explicit default: label",
    "suggestions": [],
    "facts": {"enum":"Color","missingCount":"1","missingValues":"BLUE"},
    "relatedLocations": [],
    "fixes": []
  }
]
    @endcode

    @section diag_code_frames Code Frames

    With <tt>--code-frames</tt>, %Qore renders each diagnostic to \c stderr as a
    code frame: a <tt>file:line:column</tt> header tagged with the severity and
    stable code, the offending source line, and a caret span (<tt>^~~~</tt>)
    underlining the reported columns. This is useful for human review and for tools
    that surface compiler output directly. For the \c bad.q example above:

    @code{.sh}
qore --code-frames bad.q
    @endcode

    @verbatim
bad.q:8:12: error[PARSE-EXCEPTION]: reference to undefined class 'widget'; did you mean 'Widget'? (check capitalization)
  Widget w = new widget();
              ^~~~~~~~~~~~
bad.q:9: error[PARSE-EXCEPTION]: function 'calculateTotl()' cannot be found; did you mean 'calculateTotal'?
  int result = calculateTotl(2, 3);
    @endverbatim

    When a diagnostic's columns are unknown (column \c -1) the header omits the
    column and no caret span is drawn, as in the second frame above.

    @note Structured diagnostics are also available to embedded code via the
    @ref Qore::Program::setParseDiagnosticsCollected() "Program::setParseDiagnosticsCollected()"
    and @ref Qore::Program::getParseDiagnostics() "Program::getParseDiagnostics()"
    methods, which return @ref Qore::ParseDiagnosticInfo "ParseDiagnosticInfo"
    hashes equivalent to the JSON above.

    There are two additional options available with debugging versions of %Qore as follows:

    <b>Description of Debugging Command-Line Parameters</b>
    |!Long Param|!Short|!Description
    |<tt>--debug=</tt><em>arg</em>|\c -d|Turns on %Qore debugging output (output to \c stderr). Higher arg numbers give more output. This option is only available with DEBUG builds
    |<tt>--trace</tt>|\c -t|Turns on %Qore tracing (output to \c stderr). This option is only available with DEBUG builds

    @section repl_mode Interactive REPL Mode

    When %Qore is started without a script file and standard input is a terminal, it automatically enters
    interactive REPL (Read-Eval-Print Loop) mode. This provides an interactive shell for experimenting
    with %Qore code, testing expressions, and exploring the language.

    @code{.sh}
    $ qore
    Qore 2.0.0 Interactive Shell
    Type /help for commands, /quit to exit
    qore> 1 + 1
    => 2
    qore> our string name = "World"
    qore> printf("Hello, %s!\n", name)
    Hello, World!
    qore>
    @endcode

    @subsection repl_linenoise Line Editing and History (linenoise)

    When the \c linenoise module is installed, the REPL provides enhanced line editing capabilities
    similar to those in other shells like bash. This includes:

    - Arrow key navigation within the current line
    - Command history navigation with Up/Down arrows
    - Reverse history search with Ctrl+R
    - Tab completion for REPL commands

    <b>Keyboard Shortcuts</b> (available when linenoise is installed)
    |!Shortcut|!Description
    |Up/Down|Navigate through command history
    |Left/Right|Move cursor within the current line
    |Ctrl+A|Move cursor to beginning of line
    |Ctrl+E|Move cursor to end of line
    |Ctrl+R|Reverse search through history
    |Tab|Complete REPL commands (commands starting with \c /)
    |Ctrl+C|Cancel current input; if at prompt, exit the REPL

    <b>Persistent History</b>

    When linenoise is available, the REPL automatically saves command history to
    \c ~/.qore-repl-history (or \c %USERPROFILE%\\.qore-repl-history on Windows).
    History is loaded when the REPL starts and saved when it exits, so your
    command history persists across sessions.

    @note The linenoise module is optional. If it is not installed, the REPL
    will still work but without line editing, history navigation, or tab completion.
    Install the linenoise module for the best interactive experience.

    @subsection repl_commands REPL Commands

    The REPL supports special commands that start with a forward slash (\c /). Type \c /help to see all
    available commands.

    <b>Basic Commands</b>
    |!Command|!Description
    |\c /help|Display help information about all available commands
    |\c /quit|Exit the REPL (aliases: \c /exit, \c /q)

    <b>Session Commands</b>
    |!Command|!Description
    |\c /reset|Reset the program state, clearing all defined variables, functions, and classes
    |\c /clear|Clear pending multi-line input without executing it
    |\c /history|Display the command history for the current session
    |\c /timing|Toggle execution timing display; when enabled, shows how long each command takes to execute

    <b>Introspection Commands</b>
    |!Command|!Description
    |\c /vars|List all user-defined global variables and their values
    |\c /functions|List all user-defined functions (alias: \c /funcs)
    |\c /classes|List all user-defined classes
    |\c /constants|List all user-defined constants (alias: \c /consts)
    |\c /namespaces|List all namespaces (alias: \c /ns)
    |\c /modules|List all loaded modules
    |<tt>/type </tt><em>expr</em>|Show the type of an expression or variable
    |<tt>/describe </tt><em>name</em>|Show detailed information about a class, function, or namespace

    <b>File Commands</b>
    |!Command|!Description
    |<tt>/load </tt><em>file</em>|Load and execute a %Qore source file
    |<tt>/save </tt><em>file</em>|Save the session history (code only, not REPL commands) to a file
    |\c /pwd|Show the current working directory
    |<tt>/cd </tt><em>dir</em>|Change the current working directory

    @subsection repl_multiline Multi-line Input

    The REPL automatically detects when more input is needed for multi-line statements. The prompt
    changes from \c "qore>" to \c "...>" to indicate that the REPL is waiting for more input:

    @code{.sh}
    qore> class Person {
    ...>     public { string name; int age; }
    ...>     constructor(string n, int a) {
    ...>         name = n;
    ...>         age = a;
    ...>     }
    ...> }
    qore> Person p("Alice", 30)
    qore> printf("%s is %d years old\n", p.name, p.age)
    Alice is 30 years old
    @endcode

    Use the \c /clear command to cancel multi-line input if you make a mistake.

    @subsection repl_examples REPL Examples

    <b>Defining and using functions:</b>
    @code{.sh}
    qore> sub greet(string name) { return "Hello, " + name + "!"; }
    qore> greet("World")
    => "Hello, World!"
    qore> /functions
    User-defined functions:
      greet()
    @endcode

    <b>Exploring types:</b>
    @code{.sh}
    qore> /type 42
    Type: integer
    qore> /type "hello"
    Type: string
    qore> /type (1, 2, 3)
    Type: list
    qore> /type {"name": "Alice", "age": 30}
    Type: hash
    @endcode

    <b>Describing classes:</b>
    @code{.sh}
    qore> class Counter { private { int n = 0; } int inc() { return ++n; } int get() { return n; } }
    qore> /describe Counter
    Class: Counter
      Methods:
        inc()
        get()
      Members:
        n
    @endcode

    <b>Using timing to measure performance:</b>
    @code{.sh}
    qore> /timing
    Timing display enabled
    qore> int sum = 0; for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) sum += i;
    => 499999500000
       (00:00:00.15)
    @endcode

    <b>Saving your session:</b>
    @code{.sh}
    qore> our int x = 42
    qore> sub double(int n) { return n * 2; }
    qore> /save /tmp/my_session.q
    Session saved to: /tmp/my_session.q
    @endcode

    @subsection repl_tips Tips for Using the REPL

    - Use \c our to declare global variables that persist across statements
    - Use \c sub (not \c int \c sub) for simple function definitions at the top level
    - Class members must be declared in \c public or \c private blocks
    - The REPL maintains state between commands, so you can build up complex programs incrementally
    - Use \c /reset to start fresh if you want to clear all definitions
    - Use \c /save to save your work before exiting
    - Install the \c linenoise module for enhanced line editing and command history

    @see \c QoreRepl::QoreRepl for programmatic REPL access
*/
